<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <SharedKernelTier>Foundation</SharedKernelTier>
    <!--
      Ship the XML documentation alongside the assembly. This is NOT redundant with
      Directory.Build.targets' own default: the .NET SDK assigns GenerateDocumentationFile=false
      early, so that file's '== empty string' guard never fires and no package in this repo shipped
      a .xml until each set it explicitly. Without this element the nupkg contains
      lib/net10.0/SharedKernel.Primitives.dll and no .xml, and every consumer of the platform's
      most-depended-upon types loses IntelliSense on all of them.
    -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!-- NuGet packaging metadata -->
    <Description>Foundational primitives for the SharedKernel: Result&lt;T&gt;, Error, ErrorCodes, IClock, SmartEnum, ValidationResult, IIdGenerator, and the WellKnownHeaders/BaggageKeys/TagKeys propagation registries. Carries one first-party Microsoft.Extensions dependency (Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions), used solely by the optional ClockExtensions.AddClock() DI convenience extension. Trim- and AOT-clean: compiles with zero IL2026/IL3050/IL2059 under EnableTrimAnalyzer and EnableAotAnalyzer.</Description>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>This package ships as part of the platform's single, repo-wide MinVer-derived version (see root CLAUDE.md's "Package Versioning" section) — the notes below describe what changed and why, not a package-specific version number.

ADDITION (P-552): Error.MessageArguments, the values for the named placeholders of a translated message. SharedKernel.Localization fills it when an error is built from a LocalizedMessage definition, and the HTTP boundary uses it to translate the message with the same values. It is empty for every factory in this package, not part of equality or the hash code, and not serialized (Message already holds the values in the default text), so existing errors, comparisons and JSON payloads are unchanged.

PRE-FIRST-PUBLISH HARDENING PASS. Six defects were found by executing the compiled assembly rather than reading it, and fixed while this package was still unpublished and therefore still free to change. Four of them contradicted the affected type's own XML documentation, which has been corrected in each case.

BEHAVIOUR CHANGES (all of them turn a silent wrong answer into a loud one; none affects a correctly-written call site):
1. Result.Error no longer returns null for an uninitialized default(Result). A struct instance that never ran Success()/Failure() reported IsFailure=true while holding no error, so Result.Error returned null in violation of Error's own "never null" contract and surfaced as a NullReferenceException at whatever unrelated call site touched it next — typically Error.ToProblemDetails() at the HTTP boundary. Reachable without writing "default" at all: the out-parameter of a failed Dictionary.TryGetValue, an element of new Result[n], or an unassigned field. It now throws InvalidOperationException naming default(Result) as the cause.
2. ValidationResult and ValidationResult&lt;T&gt; now compare by value. Both are declared "record", but their IReadOnlyList&lt;Error&gt; member made the compiler-generated equality fall back to reference equality, so two failures built from equal errors compared unequal while two successes compared equal (both share one static empty array) — equality that worked or broke depending on the outcome.
3. ValidationResult and ValidationResult&lt;T&gt; now snapshot the supplied error sequence. The caller's own IReadOnlyList was stored by reference, so mutating it afterwards mutated the "immutable" record: clearing it produced a failed result carrying zero errors, precisely the state Failure()'s ArgumentException guard exists to reject.
4. Result.Failure, Result&lt;T&gt;.Failure, and both implicit Error conversions now reject a null error instead of constructing defect 1 deliberately. ValidationResult.Failure additionally rejects a null element.
5. SmartEnum.TryFromValue returns false for a null lookup key instead of throwing ArgumentNullException out of Dictionary internals — a Try-shaped member must not throw for a missing key. FromValue/FromName now validate their arguments themselves rather than leaking a "Parameter: key" message from an internal collection. A new TryFromName completes the pair.
6. SmartEnum reports duplicate member values and names as an InvalidOperationException naming the type, the duplicated key, and both colliding member names. Previously a duplicate surfaced as ArgumentException "An item with the same key has already been added. Key: 1" from inside a Lazy, at whichever call site happened to look up first.

ADDITIONS:
- Error.Details and Error.Validation(IReadOnlyList&lt;Error&gt;). One failed Result can now carry every invalid field: the aggregate error has code ErrorCodes.Validation.Failed ("validation.failed"), type Validation, and the individual errors in Details, compared by value. Details is empty for every other factory, so existing errors and their equality are unchanged. Details now also round-trips through System.Text.Json's default reflection-based serializer (via [JsonInclude] on its private init accessor), including a nested Error inside Details; an absent or null "details" member deserializes to an empty list, never null — a serialized Error previously lost every child error crossing a process boundary (an HTTP response, an idempotency-replay payload) because the property had no way for the default serializer to read it back.
- SmartEnum implements IComparable&lt;TEnum&gt; and IComparable, ordering by the underlying value, so List&lt;TEnum&gt;.Sort(), OrderBy, and SortedSet all work; previously Sort() threw InvalidOperationException. This widens the TValue constraint to IEquatable&lt;TValue&gt; + IComparable&lt;TValue&gt;; every SmartEnum in this repo uses int and is unaffected, but a TValue without IComparable&lt;TValue&gt; will no longer satisfy the constraint.
- SmartEnumJsonConverter&lt;TEnum, TValue&gt;, serializing a member as its underlying value and reading it back through TryFromValue. A SmartEnum was previously write-only over JSON: the default object serializer emitted {"Name":...,"Value":...} and could not read it back, because members are singletons behind a private constructor. Opt-in per property or per options instance; no JsonSerializerContext is shipped and no global default is touched.
- ErrorCodes.Forbidden (Default, InsufficientPermission), closing the gap left when ErrorType.Forbidden shipped in WO-059/P-384 without companion code constants.
- WellKnownBaggageKeys.TenantId. Deliberately "TenantId", matching what SharedKernel.MultiTenancy already writes, NOT "tenant.id" for symmetry with WellKnownTagKeys.TenantId: BaggageLogRecordProcessor copies baggage generically, so this string is the emitted log property name and renaming it would break deployed dashboards and alert rules.

TRIMMING AND AOT: this package advertised "AOT-compatible" while emitting a real IL2059 trim warning from SmartEnum into every trimming consumer's build — a hard failure under TreatWarningsAsErrors — and its own XML doc asserted no such warning could occur. A trimmed TrimMode=full publish confirmed the mechanism is nonetheless correct, so the call now carries an UnconditionalSuppressMessage stating why the suppression is sound, and the doc says what was measured instead of what was assumed. The package now compiles with zero IL2026/IL3050/IL2059 under both EnableTrimAnalyzer and EnableAotAnalyzer.

XML DOCUMENTATION is now shipped in the package for the first time. GenerateDocumentationFile had to be set per-project: the SDK assigns it false early enough that Directory.Build.targets' empty-string-guarded default never applied, so the nupkg previously contained the assembly and no .xml.

Notable change (WO-059 / P-384): added ErrorType.Forbidden and the corresponding Error.Forbidden(string code, string message) factory, mirroring BusinessRule's addition pattern (P-042). Forbidden represents a caller who is generally permitted to attempt an operation, but for whom this specific instance/condition is not satisfied — maps to HTTP 403 Forbidden at the presentation layer. Distinct from Unauthorized (not permitted to attempt this at all — HTTP 401) and from BusinessRule (a domain invariant violation, not an authorization/permission gate). Purely additive: no existing ErrorType member's numeric value changed and no existing Error factory's signature changed. COMPILE-TIME RISK: a downstream consumer with an exhaustive switch/switch expression over ErrorType carrying no discard/default arm will fail to compile after picking up this change, because a new enum member now exists — this is a compile-time signal, not a runtime break; add a discard/default arm or an explicit Forbidden case to restore exhaustiveness. No new ErrorCodes constant was added for it — unlike BusinessRule's companion ErrorCodes.Domain.RuleViolated, no consuming phase requested a shared code constant for Forbidden.

See 01.Core/CLAUDE.md's changelog for the full history of every change to this package.</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <PackageTags>shared-kernel;result;railway-oriented;clock;smart-enum;aot</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Publish bar: fail the build on an undocumented or untracked public member. -->
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);CS1591;RS0016;RS0017;RS0024;RS0025</WarningsAsErrors>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);RS0026;RS0027</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <AdditionalFiles Include="PublicAPI.Shipped.txt" />
    <AdditionalFiles Include="PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
